  explanation:"A positive regurgitation sign on pressing over the medial canthal ligament, with recent-onset watering and recurrent conjunctivitis but no mention of a confirmatory irrigation test or of an acutely tense, hot swelling, matches the source material's first step for infective lacrimal drainage disease rather than its definitive surgical step: \"Systemic and topical antibiotics\" is listed first, before dacryocystorhinostomy, in the acute dacryocystitis treatment sequence — \"Warm compresses and nasal decongestants… Systemic and topical antibiotics… Once the infection resolves, consider dacryocystorhinostomy\" (ophthalmology.pdf p.67; Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.280). The question's own wording, \"the FIRST treatment,\" points to the first rung of this ladder rather than to its final, definitive step.\n\nProbing of the nasolacrimal duct is explicitly excluded in adults by the source's note that \"probing is not curative in adults.\" Dacryocystectomy is not the procedure the source names for lacrimal drainage disease — dacryocystorhinostomy, which restores drainage into the nose rather than removing the sac, is the one used throughout the source material. Dacryocystorhinostomy itself, while eventually appropriate, is reserved by the source for after the infection resolves, not as the first treatment step, which is what this option is being asked for.\n\nThis question shares its four-option menu with Q19 earlier in this section (antibiotics / probing / dacryocystectomy / DCR, reordered here). The discriminating token is the type of evidence given: Q19 supplies a positive lacrimal irrigation result that the source's own table maps directly onto NLD obstruction and DCR, while this vignette supplies only a history and a positive regurge test with the stem explicitly asking for the first step of management — regurge alone, without an irrigation result confirming the level of obstruction, is treated here as the acute-dacryocystitis presentation for which antibiotics come first.\n\nThe option \"Dacryosystorhinostomy\" is a printed defect for \"Dacryocystorhinostomy\" — kept verbatim, per project rule.\n\n*Written for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.*",
